window.SCHOOL_LISTENING={"../assets/media/df2101bb5ff4a52abc966a61deb5a93bc5f6f72a6909a82ae7452e6caa8c0d71.mp3":[{"id":"PPT_df2101bb5ff4a52a","audioId":"PPT_df2101bb5ff4a52a","title":"Book 2 · Unit 7 · LS · PREPARATION FOR SPEAKING","subtitle":"PREPARATION FOR SPEAKING · source slide 194","duration":108.72,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":1.64,"text":"seven .four","coverage":1},{"start":3.18,"end":6.98,"text":"My presentation is about Thomas Edison.","coverage":1},{"start":8.22,"end":13.9,"text":"He was an American inventor and he was born in 1847.","coverage":1},{"start":15.2,"end":23.21,"text":"He is a remarkable person because he invented a lot of the things we know and use every day,","coverage":1},{"start":23.94,"end":26.06,"text":"such as the electric light bulb.","coverage":1},{"start":26.62,"end":27.08,"text":"Well,","coverage":1},{"start":27.76,"end":30.28,"text":"he wasn't the first to invent one,","coverage":1},{"start":30.82,"end":36.64,"text":"but he was the first person to invent one that could last a long time.","coverage":1},{"start":37.5,"end":48.06,"text":"This was in 1879 while he was working in his research lab in Menlo Park, New Jersey in the US.","coverage":1},{"start":49.06,"end":51.84,"text":"It lasted 13 .five","coverage":1},{"start":51.84,"end":52.54,"text":"hours.","coverage":1},{"start":53.54,"end":62.34,"text":"Another important example of Edison's amazing inventions is the motion picture camera.","coverage":1},{"start":63.68,"end":67.68,"text":"We all know this today as the video camera.","coverage":1},{"start":68.88,"end":77.78,"text":"Edison and his team invented it in 1891 and they showed short six -minute films.","coverage":1},{"start":78.78,"end":83.68,"text":"Equally important was Edison's work with Henry Ford,","coverage":1},{"start":84.3,"end":87.12,"text":"who started the Ford Motor Company.","coverage":1},{"start":88.28,"end":94.54,"text":"Edison was worried about America's dependence on foreign rubber for tires.","coverage":1},{"start":95.66,"end":101.78,"text":"So while he was working at a lab in Florida in 1927,","coverage":1},{"start":102.52,"end":106.86,"text":"he found a new source of rubber in local plants.","coverage":1}],"file":"0c5b57a69d8dc198e0f9.js","track":0,"media":"../assets/media/df2101bb5ff4a52abc966a61deb5a93bc5f6f72a6909a82ae7452e6caa8c0d71.mp3"}],"../assets/media/470d1a05bf0a818b539e89bfedd471b08f0cccc82ba3634e3e6485b4d15103ee.mp3":[{"id":"PPT_470d1a05bf0a818b","audioId":"PPT_470d1a05bf0a818b","title":"Book 2 · Unit 7 · LS · LISTENING 1","subtitle":"LISTENING 1 · source slide 56","duration":361.27,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":1.9,"text":"Unit seven,","coverage":1},{"start":2.66,"end":3.6,"text":"seven .one","coverage":1},{"start":5.25,"end":5.535,"text":"Hello","coverage":1},{"start":5.535,"end":6.52,"text":"everyone.","coverage":1},{"start":6.98,"end":14.14,"text":"So in this presentation I'd like to discuss the designers of some everyday objects.","coverage":1},{"start":14.92,"end":16.38,"text":"Arnie Jakobson,","coverage":1},{"start":16.74,"end":20.68,"text":"Sir James Dyson and Sir Jonathan Ive.","coverage":1},{"start":21.68,"end":30,"text":"These people designed simple things that have changed our lives and how we do everyday things.","coverage":1},{"start":30.84,"end":34.98,"text":"Today we are going to think about these inventors and designers,","coverage":1},{"start":35.3,"end":40.28,"text":"their achievements and how their inventions and designs have helped us.","coverage":1},{"start":41.14,"end":47.9,"text":"I would like to start with Arnie Jakobson who was born in Denmark in 1902.","coverage":1},{"start":48.7,"end":56.78,"text":"When he was a child he loved to paint but his father thought being an artist wasn't a good career.","coverage":1},{"start":57.9,"end":64.46,"text":"So Jakobson became a designer of many things including buildings and furniture.","coverage":1},{"start":65.36,"end":66.2,"text":"For example,","coverage":1},{"start":66.9,"end":70.74,"text":"here is a chair he designed in 1958.","coverage":1},{"start":71.32,"end":73.84,"text":"It's called the egg chair.","coverage":1},{"start":74.58,"end":77.24,"text":"Have any of you seen this kind of chair before?","coverage":1},{"start":77.24,"end":78.6,"text":"I have.","coverage":1},{"start":79.38,"end":82.44,"text":"Did they call it the egg chair because of its shape?","coverage":1},{"start":83.06,"end":84.62,"text":"It's an interesting design.","coverage":1},{"start":85.22,"end":85.44,"text":"Yes,","coverage":1},{"start":85.84,"end":87.12,"text":"you have a good point.","coverage":1},{"start":87.84,"end":91.4,"text":"I think it was called that because of the egg -like shape.","coverage":1},{"start":92.2,"end":96.36,"text":"Nowadays we still see Jakobson's designs around us.","coverage":1},{"start":97.02,"end":98.96,"text":"When you look at the chair design,","coverage":1},{"start":99.4,"end":102.36,"text":"can you guess what Jakobson was like as a person?","coverage":1},{"start":102.36,"end":105.92,"text":"His chair looks like a simple neat design.","coverage":1},{"start":106.66,"end":111.96,"text":"So maybe he liked simple and neat things and not complicated things.","coverage":1},{"start":112.42,"end":112.8,"text":"Actually,","coverage":1},{"start":113.12,"end":113.82,"text":"that's right.","coverage":1},{"start":114.36,"end":114.96,"text":"He did.","coverage":1},{"start":115.76,"end":124.2,"text":"For example, when he was growing up he painted his room white because he didn't like all the flowers on the wallpaper he had.","coverage":1},{"start":124.78,"end":128.78,"text":"And as an adult he worked hard to make his designs perfect.","coverage":1},{"start":128.78,"end":130.46,"text":"In fact,","coverage":1},{"start":131.1,"end":136.08,"text":"he often kept his workers at work until late trying to make things better.","coverage":1},{"start":136.86,"end":138.98,"text":"But even though he worked hard,","coverage":1},{"start":139.62,"end":142.9,"text":"Jakobson liked to joke and do funny things.","coverage":1},{"start":143.42,"end":144.22,"text":"For instance,","coverage":1},{"start":144.9,"end":154.36,"text":"as a child he liked to play jokes on others and as an adult he once wore a melon for a hat when someone dared him to.","coverage":1},{"start":154.36,"end":161.84,"text":"He also liked working on his own projects in his free time and found painting to be relaxing.","coverage":1},{"start":162.9,"end":163.58,"text":"These days,","coverage":1},{"start":164.16,"end":167.5,"text":"Jakobson is famous for his modern, simple designs.","coverage":1},{"start":168.18,"end":173.66,"text":"His work showed that he thought about the purpose that his designs would serve for people.","coverage":1},{"start":174.64,"end":175.14,"text":"Next,","coverage":1},{"start":175.66,"end":177.62,"text":"I'd like to talk about an inventor,","coverage":1},{"start":178.26,"end":179.32,"text":"James Dyson.","coverage":1},{"start":179.32,"end":185.08,"text":"Can anyone think of something you often use that might have his last name,","coverage":1},{"start":185.4,"end":185.74,"text":"Dyson,","coverage":1},{"start":186.06,"end":186.64,"text":"on it?","coverage":1},{"start":186.9,"end":187.58,"text":"I know!","coverage":1},{"start":188,"end":189.24,"text":"A vacuum cleaner!","coverage":1},{"start":189.74,"end":190.74,"text":"Or a hand dryer.","coverage":1},{"start":191.16,"end":192.94,"text":"Both of those things are correct.","coverage":1},{"start":193.88,"end":198.4,"text":"Dyson's designs and inventions are made to solve problems.","coverage":1},{"start":199.16,"end":206.26,"text":"His ideas come because he sees things that could work better, like the vacuum cleaner and hand dryer.","coverage":1},{"start":206.26,"end":208.9,"text":"But how did he start?","coverage":1},{"start":209.74,"end":210.4,"text":"Well,","coverage":1},{"start":210.86,"end":214.38,"text":"Dyson was born in England in 1947.","coverage":1},{"start":215.28,"end":217.84,"text":"His father died when he was only nine.","coverage":1},{"start":218.34,"end":219.3,"text":"As a result,","coverage":1},{"start":219.92,"end":223.1,"text":"Dyson ended up becoming someone who never gave up.","coverage":1},{"start":223.88,"end":227.7,"text":"He studied art and design before he studied engineering.","coverage":1},{"start":228.86,"end":231.54,"text":"When he saw something that didn't work well,","coverage":1},{"start":231.84,"end":235.02,"text":"he got frustrated and worked to improve it.","coverage":1},{"start":235.02,"end":236.18,"text":"For example,","coverage":1},{"start":236.68,"end":241.16,"text":"his first invention was a wheelbarrow with a ball for a wheel.","coverage":1},{"start":241.8,"end":247,"text":"This made it easier to carry heavy things in the garden and over rough ground.","coverage":1},{"start":247.86,"end":250.58,"text":"It was an interesting new use for a ball.","coverage":1},{"start":251.42,"end":257.66,"text":"Also, even though he changed subjects, he was still interested in art as well as designing.","coverage":1},{"start":258.48,"end":259.8,"text":"Take a look at this photo.","coverage":1},{"start":260.38,"end":261.22,"text":"What do you see?","coverage":1},{"start":261.22,"end":264.74,"text":"It looks like a waterfall made from glass and water,","coverage":1},{"start":265.2,"end":267.74,"text":"with the water running up instead of falling down.","coverage":1},{"start":268.26,"end":269.14,"text":"That's surprising.","coverage":1},{"start":269.72,"end":270.28,"text":"That's right.","coverage":1},{"start":271.02,"end":277.24,"text":"This was a piece of art that Dyson made in 2003 called Wrong Garden.","coverage":1},{"start":278.04,"end":279.24,"text":"It tricks the eye.","coverage":1},{"start":279.9,"end":284.46,"text":"This shows Dyson's love of good design as well as being an inventor.","coverage":1},{"start":284.46,"end":288.34,"text":"Dyson is still inventing new things,","coverage":1},{"start":288.84,"end":299.26,"text":"but he is also interested in supporting education for students and gives money to schools to help students study so they might become inventors too.","coverage":1},{"start":299.88,"end":308.12,"text":"He has even donated money to the University of Cambridge to support students and research there with a focus on technology.","coverage":1},{"start":309.32,"end":312.56,"text":"Now I have one last picture to show you.","coverage":1},{"start":312.56,"end":315.86,"text":"It's a phone with a special design.","coverage":1},{"start":316.5,"end":318.3,"text":"It's called the iPhone.","coverage":1},{"start":319.16,"end":321.22,"text":"I think a lot of us know about this phone.","coverage":1},{"start":322.14,"end":325.36,"text":"It was designed by Sir Jonathan Ive for Apple.","coverage":1},{"start":326.02,"end":330.8,"text":"He is responsible for a lot of Apple's product designs, which are simple,","coverage":1},{"start":331.16,"end":332.64,"text":"clean and slim.","coverage":1},{"start":333.6,"end":337,"text":"Ive has won a lot of awards for his Apple designs,","coverage":1},{"start":337.32,"end":340.52,"text":"and you can see a lot of his work in museums too.","coverage":1},{"start":340.52,"end":348.54,"text":"He is from London and he was made a Knight Commander of the British Empire in 2012 for his design work.","coverage":1},{"start":349.62,"end":352.52,"text":"He is known as being hardworking and creative.","coverage":1},{"start":353.3,"end":359.38,"text":"I think that his designs will continue to influence the way we live and work for a long time.","coverage":1}],"file":"6b9567b9093e724cff2e.js","track":0,"media":"../assets/media/470d1a05bf0a818b539e89bfedd471b08f0cccc82ba3634e3e6485b4d15103ee.mp3"}],"../assets/media/8ace1e13194f0009c939ff6937559cbd9eaeab6f9742ac50474092f7af7b3d64.mp3":[{"id":"PPT_8ace1e13194f0009","audioId":"PPT_8ace1e13194f0009","title":"Book 2 · Unit 7 · LS · LISTENING 2","subtitle":"LISTENING 2 · source slide 89","duration":307.8,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":1.84,"text":"seven .two","coverage":1},{"start":8.06,"end":9.06,"text":"Hello","coverage":1},{"start":9.72,"end":10.01,"text":"Hi","coverage":1},{"start":10.01,"end":10.92,"text":"Yasmin,","coverage":1},{"start":11.5,"end":12.58,"text":"it's Louisa","coverage":1},{"start":12.58,"end":13.18,"text":"Hi","coverage":1},{"start":13.18,"end":13.98,"text":"Louisa,","coverage":1},{"start":14.3,"end":15.42,"text":"how are you?","coverage":1},{"start":16.06,"end":17.36,"text":"Okay, I guess","coverage":1},{"start":18.3,"end":19.12,"text":"Actually","coverage":1},{"start":20,"end":21.78,"text":"I'm not that great","coverage":1},{"start":21.78,"end":22.36,"text":"Oh","coverage":1},{"start":22.36,"end":22.72,"text":"no?","coverage":1},{"start":23.28,"end":24.24,"text":"What's up?","coverage":1},{"start":24.72,"end":25.56,"text":"Something wrong?","coverage":1},{"start":25.82,"end":28.48,"text":"It's the presentation for our course","coverage":1},{"start":29.04,"end":29.92,"text":"You know,","coverage":1},{"start":30.32,"end":35.7,"text":"the one on remarkable people who have done something really interesting or unusual","coverage":1},{"start":36.48,"end":40.88,"text":"I'm having trouble doing it Have you finished it yet?","coverage":1},{"start":41.62,"end":57.84,"text":"Yes, almost I just have to write a conclusion Good for you I bet it's really good Thanks, Louisa I'm pretty happy with it but the introduction's not great It needs a little more work","coverage":1},{"start":58.48,"end":60.08,"text":"How much have you done?","coverage":1},{"start":60.54,"end":60.98,"text":"Well","coverage":1},{"start":61.9,"end":65.16,"text":"I haven't done all of it All?","coverage":1},{"start":65.98,"end":67.16,"text":"Or any of it?","coverage":1},{"start":67.38,"end":70.92,"text":"I'm not very good at finding things to present","coverage":1},{"start":71.62,"end":74.34,"text":"I'm having trouble thinking of ideas","coverage":1},{"start":75.3,"end":77.26,"text":"Who did you do yours on?","coverage":1},{"start":77.76,"end":84,"text":"I chose Seema Najjar She's a remarkable woman Who's she?","coverage":1},{"start":84.8,"end":87.02,"text":"Why are you interested in her?","coverage":1},{"start":87.02,"end":88.32,"text":"Well,","coverage":1},{"start":88.78,"end":121.96,"text":"she's from Jordan She was working in fashion She used to be the head of a modelling company before and she worked with female Arab clothing designers So she has had a lot of success But she is famous because she started a website with videos in Arabic showing people how to do things And without even marketing the videos they started getting one million views a month How did she do that?","coverage":1},{"start":122.28,"end":122.8,"text":"Well,","coverage":1},{"start":123.2,"end":130.7,"text":"the videos are based on what people are searching for the most online They are often about fashion,","coverage":1},{"start":131.14,"end":131.58,"text":"food,","coverage":1},{"start":132.06,"end":132.48,"text":"health,","coverage":1},{"start":132.92,"end":163.02,"text":"parenting and art Many of the people who view her videos are Saudi Arabian women Another interesting thing is that she is a mother who is working full time Some of her videos help other mothers to have better health after they have their babies That's great that she has become really successful Is there anything else about her life or her company that's amazing?","coverage":1},{"start":163.42,"end":165.18,"text":"When her company started,","coverage":1},{"start":165.6,"end":175.24,"text":"most of the videos were made by people in their 20s Nowadays she has three full time workers making videos","coverage":1},{"start":175.98,"end":179.5,"text":"She says that as her company gets bigger,","coverage":1},{"start":180.04,"end":194.14,"text":"she wants to start hiring others to make videos too This will help more people get jobs That's important because a lot of young people in her area don't have enough work","coverage":1},{"start":194.94,"end":197.68,"text":"So she's an entrepreneur,","coverage":1},{"start":198.26,"end":203.4,"text":"a company leader and a working mother That's amazing","coverage":1},{"start":204.34,"end":213.26,"text":"Wow, I need to find someone like that Why don't you do something on Yoshiro Nakamatsu?","coverage":1},{"start":213.92,"end":215.58,"text":"Do you know about him?","coverage":1},{"start":215.58,"end":216.54,"text":"Um,","coverage":1},{"start":217.7,"end":222.72,"text":"I don't know a lot about Yoshiro...","coverage":1},{"start":222.72,"end":223.34,"text":"who?","coverage":1},{"start":224.42,"end":225.24,"text":"Nakamatsu","coverage":1},{"start":225.77,"end":228,"text":"Well, he was born in Japan","coverage":1},{"start":228.31,"end":235.34,"text":"He is now over 90 years old and he is still an inventor Um,","coverage":1},{"start":236.44,"end":239.64,"text":"his life doesn't sound that extraordinary","coverage":1},{"start":240.21,"end":262.26,"text":"He has created more than three ,000 inventions Including a chair that is supposed to help you think better Some of his inventions are a bit odd Like a pair of shoes made for jumping And glasses in the shape of eyes so that other people can't see them","coverage":1},{"start":262.78,"end":285.18,"text":"He even made fake hair that can help keep you safe from others I think Nakamatsu's situation is a little different from some inventors Because not everyone agrees that he has that many real inventions But he does have a lot Also,","coverage":1},{"start":285.62,"end":295.9,"text":"he didn't retire even after he reached the age of 80 You might want to look up more information about him online Okay,","coverage":1},{"start":296.82,"end":301.68,"text":"I'll check him out Do you know what the teacher wants us to present?","coverage":1},{"start":302.2,"end":302.6,"text":"Well,","coverage":1},{"start":303.36,"end":305.92,"text":"I'm not sure I understand everything","coverage":1}],"file":"b7a6482767a5f31c8068.js","track":0,"media":"../assets/media/8ace1e13194f0009c939ff6937559cbd9eaeab6f9742ac50474092f7af7b3d64.mp3"}],"../assets/media/b79dcf3754cc48196125e60d31cc6d00ec2ee773f81f37b78bc50c90d625cd24.mp3":[{"id":"PPT_b79dcf3754cc4819","audioId":"PPT_b79dcf3754cc4819","title":"Book 2 · Unit 7 · LS · LISTENING 2","subtitle":"LISTENING 2 · source slide 108","duration":26.8,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.32,"text":"seven .three","coverage":1},{"start":3.56,"end":4.44,"text":"one","coverage":1},{"start":5.04,"end":7.3,"text":"I'm pretty happy with it.","coverage":1},{"start":9.44,"end":10.14,"text":"two","coverage":1},{"start":11.02,"end":13.14,"text":"She's a remarkable woman.","coverage":1},{"start":15.44,"end":16.38,"text":"three","coverage":1},{"start":17.46,"end":18.96,"text":"That's amazing.","coverage":1},{"start":21.6,"end":22.46,"text":"four","coverage":1},{"start":23.34,"end":24.96,"text":"I'll check him out.","coverage":1}],"file":"f49e4045e560ffe3eae2.js","track":0,"media":"../assets/media/b79dcf3754cc48196125e60d31cc6d00ec2ee773f81f37b78bc50c90d625cd24.mp3"}]};
window.SCHOOL_SOURCE_ANSWERS=[{"text":"1 Where in the world is Kenya?Answer: Kenya is in Africa.","slide":4,"shape":"15","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"2 What ocean is to the east of Kenya?The Indian Ocean is to the east of Kenya.","slide":4,"shape":"17","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"3 What can you find on a beach?Answer: You can find shells and rocks, but also rubbish such as bottles.","slide":4,"shape":"5","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"1 What can you see on the beach?Possible answer: There is rubbish on the beach, including old flip-flops.","slide":6,"shape":"24","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"2 What are the people doing in the photos?Possible answer: They are picking up rubbish and making things from it.","slide":6,"shape":"25","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"3 How do you think the people of Kiwayu Island make money?Possible answer: I think they sell the things they make.","slide":6,"shape":"26","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"1  Old bottles and bags are kinds of trash.","slide":13,"shape":"8","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"2  The village is far from other places. It is isolated.","slide":13,"shape":"9","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"3  Objects can wash up when waves carry them to the beach.","slide":13,"shape":"10","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"4  People often wear flip-flops at the beach.","slide":13,"shape":"11","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"5  The artists sell ornaments to decorate homes.","slide":13,"shape":"12","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"1 There are not a lot of tourists on the beaches of Kiwayu Island.","slide":15,"shape":"13","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"2 People pick up rubbish on the beach daily to earn a living.","slide":15,"shape":"15","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"3 People turn the rubbish into art.","slide":15,"shape":"6","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"4 They make money from the tourists.","slide":15,"shape":"8","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"5 They send most of their ornaments to Nairobi, Kenya.","slide":16,"shape":"17","bookPage":"148"},{"text":"1 Who goes to the beach to pick up rubbish?Answer: e. Kiwayu women","slide":20,"shape":"13","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"2 What do people collect on the beach?Answer: a. flip-flops","slide":20,"shape":"15","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"3 Where do they take the flip-flops?Answer: h. their villages in Kiwayu","slide":20,"shape":"6","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"4 Who turns the rubbish into ornaments?Answer: d. artists and craftsmen","slide":21,"shape":"16","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"5 What do the people of Kiwayu make?Answer: f. money","slide":21,"shape":"18","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"6 Where do people sell the ornaments?Answer: g. on the internet","slide":21,"shape":"6","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"7 What did Kiwayu people not have before?Answer: c. houses","slide":22,"shape":"16","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"8 Who could not go to school before?Answer: b. some of their children","slide":22,"shape":"18","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"Why does the woman in the video say that education is the most important thing?Answer: c. Children can have a better future.","slide":25,"shape":"11","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"1 How can we stop people from putting rubbish into the oceans?Possible answer: We can make recycling easier and reward people for collecting rubbish.","slide":27,"shape":"16","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"2 Do you think turning rubbish into art is a good idea? Why / Why not?Possible answer: Yes. It gives unwanted materials another use and creates income.","slide":27,"shape":"18","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"3 What do artists and craftsmen in your country make for tourists?Possible answer: Artists in my country make hats and decorations from leaves for tourists.","slide":28,"shape":"17","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"Bottle tops and boxesPaint bottle tops to make art. Cover and paint boxes to make storage boxes.","slide":30,"shape":"13","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"Drink cans and glass bottlesUse drink cans as small plant pots and glass bottles as vases.","slide":30,"shape":"15","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"Paper bags and plastic bagsUse paper bags to cover books and weave plastic bags into a basket.","slide":30,"shape":"6","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"Plastic cups and tyresMake hanging decorations from plastic cups and furniture from tyres.","slide":31,"shape":"14","bookPage":"149"},{"text":"inventions / purposeInventions are new things. A purpose is the reason something exists or is done.","slide":44,"shape":"15","bookPage":"150"},{"text":"designs / simple / complicatedDesigns show how things are planned and made. Simple means withoutunnecessary parts; complicated means having many parts and being difficult tounderstand.","slide":44,"shape":"17","bookPage":"150"},{"text":"uses / achievement / pointsUses are ways of using things. An achievement is a success through effort. Pointsare ideas, facts or opinions people express.","slide":45,"shape":"16","bookPage":"150"},{"text":"What does an inventor do? They make new things that we never had before. Some (1) inventions are (2) simple things. For example, a smart light bulb has one (3) purpose: to light a room. It is easy to use, not (4) complicated.","slide":50,"shape":"13","bookPage":"150"},{"text":"After all, it has almost the same (5) use as a regular light bulb. When we think about the (6) design of a smart light bulb, it looks a lot like a regular one, too.","slide":50,"shape":"14","bookPage":"150"},{"text":"Sometimes, when inventors make something new, they give a presentation about it. They say what the good (7) points of the invention are and they may get a prize for their (8) achievement.","slide":51,"shape":"13","bookPage":"150"},{"text":"1 Who are some inventors and designers that you know of? What were their inventions or designs?Possible answer: Grace Hopper worked on computer programs and helped make programminglanguages easier to use.","slide":54,"shape":"16","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"2 How did their inventions or designs change lives?Possible answer: Her work helped people give computers instructions using words that were closerto human language.","slide":54,"shape":"18","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"3 Some people improve other people’s inventions. Do you think they are really inventors? Why / Why not?Possible answer: I think improving an invention can count as inventing if the person creates a newpart or a new use.","slide":55,"shape":"17","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"aThe hand dryer was designed by Sir James Dyson.","slide":58,"shape":"14","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"bThe wheelbarrow was designed by Sir James Dyson.","slide":58,"shape":"16","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"cThe iPhone was designed by Sir Jonathan Ive.","slide":58,"shape":"6","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"dThe Egg Chair was designed by Arne Jacobsen.","slide":59,"shape":"15","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"a  Hand dryer — Sir James Dyson","slide":60,"shape":"29","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"b  Wheelbarrow — Sir James Dyson","slide":60,"shape":"30","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"c  iPhone — Sir Jonathan Ive","slide":60,"shape":"31","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"d  Egg Chair — Arne Jacobsen","slide":60,"shape":"32","bookPage":"151"},{"text":"Inventor / designer\nInvention / designs\nPersonality\nWhy famous\nArne JacobsenDesigner:buildings /(1) furniture\nEgg (2) Chair;named becauseof shape\nLiked (3) simple, neat thingsWorked hard to make designs perfectMade workers work lateLiked to:• joke / do funny things• work on own projects• paint\nFamous for modern,simple designsThought about(4) purposeof his designs","slide":65,"shape":"15","bookPage":"152"},{"text":"Inventor / designer\nInvention / designs\nPersonality\nWhy famous\nSir James Dyson\nVacuum cleaner /(5) hand dryerDesigns and(6) inventionssolve problemsSees things that couldwork better\nNever gave upStudied Art / Design /EngineeringWorked to improve thingsExample: ball wheelbarrow –new (7) use for a ballInterested in art / designExample: waterfall runningup ‘Wrong Garden’\nStill inventing\nGives money to schools and university\nSupports students and research","slide":66,"shape":"16","bookPage":"152"},{"text":"Inventor / designer\nInvention / designs\nPersonality\nWhy famous\nSir Jonathan Ive\n(8) iPhoneDesigned products for Apple – simple, clean, slim\nMade Knight Commander of the British Empire in 2012\nHardworking and creative\n(9) Designs will continue to influence how we live and work","slide":67,"shape":"15","bookPage":"152"},{"text":"1. The presentation introduces designers whose objects have changed how people live and do everyday things.","slide":70,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"11. Why is the chair called the Egg Chair?\nA. Its original colours resembled an egg.\nB. Its designer took the name from a childhood story.\nC. Its shape is like an egg.","slide":70,"shape":"8","bookPage":null},{"text":"retire / entrepreneursRetire means stop working, usually at an older age. Entrepreneurs start their ownbusinesses.","slide":84,"shape":"15","bookPage":"158"},{"text":"reach / situations / successReach means arrive at a place, age or point. Situations are the conditions at a timeand place. Success means a good result.","slide":84,"shape":"17","bookPage":"158"},{"text":"amazing / remarkableAmazing means very surprising. Remarkable means unusual in a way that peopleadmire.","slide":85,"shape":"16","bookPage":"158"},{"text":"Young entrepreneurs can try their ideas before they reach the age of 30. They may learnto deal with difficult situations and make their own decisions. If their business is asuccess, they can create jobs for other people.","slide":87,"shape":"9","bookPage":"158"},{"text":"1 Who are some remarkable people that you admire? Why?Possible answer: I admire Roy Allela because he invented smart gloves that help people who cannothear communicate with others.","slide":89,"shape":"13","bookPage":"158"},{"text":"2 Would you like to be an entrepreneur and start your own business? Why / Why not?Possible answer: I would like to start a business making reusable bags because I enjoy design andwant to reduce waste.","slide":89,"shape":"15","bookPage":"158"},{"text":"Person 1: name and jobSima Najjar is an entrepreneur. She was head of a modelling company and workedwith clothing designers.","slide":92,"shape":"14","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"Person 1: why remarkableShe started a website with videos in Arabic showing people how to do things.","slide":92,"shape":"16","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"Person 2: name and jobYoshiro Nakamatsu is an inventor from Japan.","slide":92,"shape":"6","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"Person 2: why remarkableIn the conversation, he is over 90 and still inventing, with more than 3,000 reportedinventions.","slide":93,"shape":"15","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"\nPerson 1\nPerson 2\nName\nSima Najjar\nYoshiro Nakamatsu\nJob\nEntrepreneur; former head of a modelling company\nInventor from Japan\nWhy remarkable\nStarted a website with videos in Arabic showing people how to do things.\nOver 90 and still inventing; more than 3,000 reported inventions.","slide":94,"shape":"14","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"1 What work do Luisa and Yasmin have to do?Answer: They have to prepare a presentation on a remarkable person.","slide":96,"shape":"14","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"2 Who has done most of their work?Answer: Yasmin has done most of her work.","slide":96,"shape":"16","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"1  Actually, I’m not that great.","slide":98,"shape":"7","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"2  Well … I haven’t done all of it.","slide":98,"shape":"8","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"3  I’m not very good at finding things to present.","slide":98,"shape":"9","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"4  I don’t know a lot about Yoshiro … .","slide":98,"shape":"10","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"1  I’m having trouble doing it. Answer: L. Luisa","slide":100,"shape":"6","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"2  The introduction’s not great. Answer: Y. Yasmin","slide":100,"shape":"7","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"3  It needs a little more work. Answer: Y. Yasmin","slide":100,"shape":"8","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"4  His life doesn’t sound that extraordinary. Answer: L. Luisa","slide":100,"shape":"9","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"5  Well, I’m not sure I understand everything. Answer: L. Luisa","slide":100,"shape":"10","bookPage":"159"},{"text":"Which sentences use a positive adjective with a negative verb?The introduction’s not great.His life doesn’t sound that extraordinary.","slide":102,"shape":"12","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"Which sentence weakens a negative statement using a phrase?It needs a little more work.","slide":102,"shape":"14","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"1 Luisa is feeling bad.","slide":104,"shape":"12","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"2 Luisa hasn’t prepared her presentation.","slide":104,"shape":"13","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"3 Luisa is bad at finding things to present.","slide":105,"shape":"12","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"4 Luisa doesn’t know anything about Yoshiro Nakamatsu.","slide":105,"shape":"14","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"1My introduction isn’t very interesting.","slide":107,"shape":"13","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"2My presentation needs a little more preparation.","slide":107,"shape":"15","bookPage":"160"},{"text":"1 I’m pretty happy with it.Enthusiastic","slide":110,"shape":"14","bookPage":"161"},{"text":"2 She’s a remarkable woman.Enthusiastic","slide":110,"shape":"15","bookPage":"161"},{"text":"3 That’s amazing!Enthusiastic","slide":110,"shape":"16","bookPage":"161"},{"text":"4 I’ll check him out.Not enthusiastic","slide":110,"shape":"17","bookPage":"161"},{"text":"Enthusiastic deliveryRaise your voice or say the emotion word more loudly.","slide":112,"shape":"12","bookPage":"161"},{"text":"Unenthusiastic deliveryKeep your voice flat, lower it or say the emotion word more quietly.","slide":112,"shape":"14","bookPage":"161"},{"text":"1 It’s an interesting design.","slide":117,"shape":"12","bookPage":"153"},{"text":"2 He found painting to be relaxing.","slide":117,"shape":"14","bookPage":"153"},{"text":"3 He got frustrated and worked to improve it.","slide":118,"shape":"12","bookPage":"153"},{"text":"4 But he is also interested in supporting education.","slide":118,"shape":"14","bookPage":"153"},{"text":"1a We were excited by the prize for our achievement.","slide":121,"shape":"12","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"1b The prize for our achievement was exciting.","slide":121,"shape":"13","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"2a Work today was very frustrating.","slide":121,"shape":"14","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"2b My work went poorly today, and I was frustrated.","slide":121,"shape":"5","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"3a The invention was surprising to me.","slide":121,"shape":"6","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"3b I was surprised by the invention.","slide":121,"shape":"7","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"4a My colleague was interested in the points I made during the meeting.","slide":121,"shape":"8","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"4b My colleague also made some very interesting points during our discussion.","slide":122,"shape":"13","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"1 What is something that you think is boring?","slide":124,"shape":"12","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"2 When was the last time that you were frustrated? What happened?","slide":124,"shape":"14","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"3 What types of stories do you think are interesting?","slide":125,"shape":"12","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"4 What was relaxing for you when you were a child?","slide":125,"shape":"14","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"What is something that you think is boring? Possible answer: I think waiting in a long queue is boring.","slide":126,"shape":"7","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"When was the last time that you were frustrated? What happened? Possible answer: I was frustrated yesterday because my computer stopped working.","slide":126,"shape":"14","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"What types of stories do you think are interesting? Possible answer: I think stories about unusual inventions are interesting.","slide":126,"shape":"5","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"What was relaxing for you when you were a child? Possible answer: Drawing pictures was relaxing for me when I was a child.","slide":127,"shape":"13","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"When he was very young, he became (1) fascinated with inventing. His parents were (2) interested in his inventions. His mother even helped him to register them.","slide":133,"shape":"16","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"Nakamatsu went to university and studied Engineering. He continued to be (3) excited about inventing, and made over 3,000 inventions. Some people say it is closer to 4,000.","slide":133,"shape":"17","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"Some of his inventions are very (4) surprising, and include a pair of shoes for jumping and a chair that helps you think.","slide":133,"shape":"5","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"Nakamatsu never got (5) tired of inventing, even when he was in his 90s. In fact, there are films about him and he sometimes appeared on TV shows.","slide":134,"shape":"16","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"He made people laugh and was not (6) boring to watch or listen to. In his opinion, people can live to be 144 years old if they take care of their health.","slide":134,"shape":"17","bookPage":"154"},{"text":"1At 7 am, I was eating breakfast.","slide":140,"shape":"13","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"2At 8 am, I was walking to the bus stop.","slide":140,"shape":"15","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"3At 8.30 am, I was reading on the bus.","slide":140,"shape":"6","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"4At 9 am, I wasn’t watching television.","slide":141,"shape":"14","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"A questionWere you eating breakfast at 7 am?","slide":143,"shape":"16","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"A positive answerYes, I was. I was eating toast with my family.","slide":143,"shape":"18","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"A negative answerNo, I wasn’t. I was still sleeping at 7 am.","slide":143,"shape":"6","bookPage":"155"},{"text":"In 1895, German scientist Wilhelm Röntgen (1) was experimenting with electricity when he (2) noticed something strange about a piece of equipment which created green lights around some objects.","slide":156,"shape":"15","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"While he (3) was watching the experiment, the rays of the green lights (4) went through paper, but not thick objects, like bones. On this day, Röntgen (5) discovered X-rays.","slide":156,"shape":"16","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"In 1945, American engineer Percy Spencer (6) was working in a lab. One day, while he (7) was doing an experiment, something amazing (8) happened.","slide":156,"shape":"17","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"While he (9) was waiting for the experiment to heat up, he (10) realized that a chocolate bar had melted in his pocket. Spencer (11) was very surprised.","slide":156,"shape":"5","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"The next day, he (12) brought an egg to the lab. While another scientist (13) was doing the experiment, the egg (14) blew up in his face.","slide":157,"shape":"15","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"On this day, Spencer accidentally (15) invented the microwave oven.","slide":157,"shape":"16","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"In 1908, a German woman named Melitta Bentz (16) made an accidental invention.","slide":157,"shape":"17","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"While she (17) was making a cup of coffee, she (18) discovered that paper stopped coffee granules from getting into her cup. On this day, she (19) created the coffee filter.","slide":157,"shape":"5","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"(1) When / While Lina (2) was doing research for her class, she (3) saw an email from a woman in Japan. The woman had Lina’s surname.","slide":161,"shape":"13","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"(4) When / While she (5) was reading the message about the woman’s family history, she quickly (6) realized that they were cousins.","slide":161,"shape":"14","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"Last year, Sultan (7) was working in his office (8) when he (9) dropped his phone.","slide":162,"shape":"13","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"He (10) was looking under his desk for his phone (11) when he suddenly (12) found 500 euros.","slide":162,"shape":"14","bookPage":"157"},{"text":"I was excited.","slide":166,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"We were making a poster.","slide":166,"shape":"9","bookPage":null},{"text":"I had an idea while I was doing my homework. First, I noticed a problem with my desk. Then I drew a quick design. After that, I showed the design to my brother.","slide":167,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"Our class was getting ready for a science display. First, we chose a large sheet of paper. We added three facts about the invention. At the end, we put the poster on the wall.","slide":168,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"A woman is working at a table in a workshop. She is testing a white kettle. She seems interested in its design.","slide":169,"shape":"7","bookPage":null},{"text":"There are tools hanging on the wall and a meter on the table. The woman is using the meter near the kettle. The task looks complicated to me.","slide":170,"shape":"7","bookPage":null},{"text":"1. The talk says that inventions and designs have helped us.","slide":175,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"11. How did Jacobsen’s father’s opinion affect his career direction?\nA. He chose painting instead of designing buildings.\nB. He stopped working on furniture to become an artist.\nC. He moved from an interest in painting into design.","slide":175,"shape":"8","bookPage":null},{"text":"1. The presentation concerns designers whose work affects everyday life.\nAnswer: TRUE","slide":178,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"11. The waterfall artwork is described as appearing to send water upwards.\nAnswer: TRUE","slide":178,"shape":"8","bookPage":null},{"text":"The job was tiring.","slide":187,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"We were discussing a solution.","slide":187,"shape":"9","bookPage":null},{"text":"We changed our plan after we saw the first result. First, the original product did not work. Then we tested the new design. After that, we explained the change to the class.","slide":188,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"I learned why this young entrepreneur is successful. She first had an idea for a useful product. While she was studying, she made small bags at home. Now she sells the bags online.","slide":189,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"A woman is packing an item into a cardboard box. Boxes, scissors, and string are on the table. She may be an entrepreneur preparing an order.","slide":190,"shape":"7","bookPage":null},{"text":"The woman can use the scissors and string to close the box. A laptop is open beside her. The purpose may be to send a product to a customer.","slide":191,"shape":"7","bookPage":null},{"text":"Company leader — a. employs young people","slide":196,"shape":"12","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Fashion — b. head of modelling company","slide":196,"shape":"14","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Fashion — d. worked with female Arab clothing designers","slide":196,"shape":"6","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Videos — c. ‘how to’ videos","slide":196,"shape":"8","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Videos — f. viewed by many Saudi Arabian women","slide":197,"shape":"15","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Why is she famous? — g. entrepreneur: started an online company with videos in Arabic","slide":197,"shape":"17","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Personal life — e. mother","slide":197,"shape":"6","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"a employs young people","slide":198,"shape":"66","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"b head of modelling company","slide":198,"shape":"75","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"d worked with female Arab clothing designers","slide":198,"shape":"81","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"c ‘how to’ videos","slide":198,"shape":"94","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"f viewed by many Saudi Arabian women","slide":198,"shape":"98","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"g entrepreneur: started an online company with videos in Arabic","slide":198,"shape":"115","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"e mother","slide":198,"shape":"128","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"IncludeI would explain how she started making online videos because this is a mainreason she became famous and a large part of her business.","slide":201,"shape":"13","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Leave out or shortenI would give less detail about her personal life unless it explains her work or itsimpact.","slide":201,"shape":"15","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Invented Sign-IO:\nsmart gloves for\nmaking sign\nlanguage into\nspeech","slide":204,"shape":"15","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Won an award; helps\nKenyan children","slide":204,"shape":"18","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Kenyan","slide":204,"shape":"24","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"His niece could not\nhear; her family\ncould not understand\nsign language","slide":204,"shape":"27","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Worked for Intel","slide":204,"shape":"33","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Tutored at Oxford\nUniversity","slide":204,"shape":"36","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Gloves in different\ncolours","slide":204,"shape":"42","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Children like the\ngloves because they\nlook cool","slide":204,"shape":"45","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"IncludeI want to include why Roy Allela invented the gloves and how they help people.","slide":206,"shape":"13","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"Leave out or shortenI don’t think we need much information about the colours of the gloves.","slide":206,"shape":"15","bookPage":"163"},{"text":"(1) Nowadays, many women are doctors, inventors and scientists. Fe del Mundo was all three. She was born in the Philippines (2) in 1911.","slide":211,"shape":"17","bookPage":"164"},{"text":"(3) At that time children sometimes died young. Fe had a sister who wanted to be a doctor, but Fe’s sister died. (4) After that, Fe decided to be a doctor.","slide":211,"shape":"18","bookPage":"164"},{"text":"(5) When Fe grew up, she got a medical degree. (6) Then, in 1936, she wrote to Harvard University to say that she wanted to study to be a doctor there.","slide":212,"shape":"17","bookPage":"164"},{"text":"(7) Before 1936, Harvard did not let women study to be doctors, but they said yes to Fe.","slide":212,"shape":"18","bookPage":"164"},{"text":"(8) After Fe became a doctor, she returned to the Philippines. One of her inventions was an incubator, which kept tiny new babies warm.","slide":213,"shape":"17","bookPage":"164"},{"text":"(9) In 1957, she sold her house and used the money to build a hospital for children. During her life, Fe del Mundo wrote over 100 research papers. She was still working when she was in her 90s. She died in 2011.","slide":213,"shape":"18","bookPage":"164"},{"text":"He is a remarkable person because he invented a lot of the things we know and use every day, (1) such as the electric light bulb.","slide":219,"shape":"18","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"(2) Another important example of Edison’s amazing inventions is the motion picture camera. We all know this today as the video camera.","slide":219,"shape":"19","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"(3) Equally important was Edison’s work with Henry Ford, who started the Ford Motor Company. Edison was worried about America’s dependence on foreign rubber for tyres.","slide":219,"shape":"20","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"This was in 1879, while he was working in his research lab in Menlo Park, New Jersey, in the US.","slide":221,"shape":"12","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"So while he was working at a lab in Florida in 1927, he found a new source of rubber in local plants.","slide":221,"shape":"14","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"1 What type of information did the student include about Thomas Edison? Why?Possible answer: The student mostly included inventions because they are relevant to Edison’sachievements and impact.","slide":227,"shape":"16","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"2 What other information would you include?Possible answer: I would include more about his younger years.","slide":227,"shape":"18","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"3 What information was interesting or surprising?Possible answer: I was surprised that he worked on rubber for tyres with Henry Ford.","slide":228,"shape":"17","bookPage":"165"},{"text":"My presentation is about Roy Allela, an inventor from Kenya. He is remarkable becausehe helped his niece to communicate with her family. When Allela’s niece was born, shecould not hear. She learned sign language, but her family couldn’t understand her.","slide":239,"shape":"9","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"In 2019, Allela was working for Intel and was tutoring for Oxford University. At that time,he invented smart gloves, called Sign-IO. The gloves could connect to a mobile phoneand change sign language into speech that others could hear. After that, Allela’s familycould talk to his niece more easily.","slide":240,"shape":"9","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"Then Allela tested the gloves with many children who could not hear. Next, he improvedthe Sign-IO gloves so the voices used for the speech sounded like boys and girls. Finally,he made the gloves’ design look cool so children would enjoy wearing them.","slide":241,"shape":"9","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"Because of his invention, Allela won an award and prize money. He wanted to use theprize to help all Kenyan children to be able to talk to others easily.","slide":242,"shape":"9","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"A: Mr Edison is the most remarkable person because one of his inventions changed theworld.","slide":244,"shape":"11","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"B: For example, he developed an electric light bulb that lasted a long time.","slide":244,"shape":"13","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"C: That’s interesting. But I think Sima Najjar has more impact because her videos getone million views a month in the example we studied.","slide":244,"shape":"6","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"B: I agree that this is remarkable. Nowadays, it’s important to think about how new ideashelp people.","slide":245,"shape":"12","bookPage":"166"},{"text":"While she was testing the device, it stopped.","slide":249,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"After that, he designed a simple solution.","slide":249,"shape":"9","bookPage":null},{"text":"The order of these events explains why the invention mattered. First, Lina noticed water was being wasted. While she was studying, she designed a new tap. Later, the tap helped her school save water.","slide":250,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"I chose only the details that help listeners understand the person. He began by fixing bicycles for neighbours. He later started a class to share those repair skills. Now local children use the skills from that class to fix their own bikes.","slide":251,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"A runner is in a starting position on a red track. Metal seats and mountains are behind her. Training for a race can be tiring, but reaching the start is an achievement.","slide":252,"shape":"7","bookPage":null},{"text":"The runner is preparing to start on the track. She may first run a short distance. After that, she could return to the starting area and practise again.","slide":253,"shape":"7","bookPage":null}];
